56	
57	\begin{document}
58	
59	\section{Digital Interface}
60	\begin{center}
61	\begin{tabular}{ c|c|c }
62	Pin & Direction & Function\\
63	 \hline
64	SCK & in & SCK for SPI communication/SCK for PLL communication\\
65	MOSI & in & MOSI for SPI communication/MOSI for PLL communication\\
66	MISO & out & MISO for SPI communication/MUX for PLL communication\\
67	NSS & in & Chip Select for SPI communication/LE for PLL communication\\
68	INTR & out & Active high interrupt indicator\\
69	RESET\footnotemark & in & FPGA reset\\
70	AUX1 & in & Selector for direct communication with Source PLL\\
71	AUX2 & in & Selector for direct communication with LO PLL\\
72	AUX3 & in & Active low sweep enable. Has to be high when changing settings\\
73	Trigger In\footnotemark & in & Trigger input for synchronization across devices\\
74	Trigger Out\footnotemark & Out & Trigger output for synchronization across devices\\
75	\end{tabular}
76	\end{center}
77	\footnotetext[1]{Reset is named "MCU\_FPGA\_UNUSED1" in the schematic as this is a later software addition}
78	\footnotetext[2]{Trigger In is named "MCU\_FPGA\_UNUSED2" in the schematic as this is a later software addition}
79	\footnotetext[3]{Trigger Out is named "MCU\_FPGA\_UNUSED3" in the schematic as this is a later software addition}
80	Depending on the voltage on AUX1/AUX2 the SPI port controls either the FPGA or one of the MAX2871 PLLs:
81	\begin{center}
82	\begin{tabular}{ c|c|c }
83	AUX1 & AUX2 & Function\\
84	 \hline
85	low & low & SPI communication with FPGA\\
86	high & low & Direct feedthrough of SCK, MOSI, MISO and NSS to Source PLL\\
87	low & high & Direct feedthrough of SCK, MOSI, MISO and NSS to LO PLL\\
88	high & high & Invalid\\
89	\end{tabular}
90	\end{center}
91	When communicating with a PLL, the MUX output of the MAX2871 is forwarded to MISO and the NSS signal is forwarded to the LE pin. As the LE pin should stay low until after a valid register has been shifted in (see MAX2871 datasheet), set NSS low before switching to PLL communication mode.
92	
93	\section{SPI Protocol}
94	Each SPI transfer starts with pulling NSS low and ends with NSS returning to high level. SPI communication is done in words of 16\,bits. The first word after NSS is pulled low is the command word and determines the amount and meaning of the following words.
95	The word received while transmitting the command word is the interrupt status register:
96	\begin{center}
97	\begin{tikzpicture}
98	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
99	\robits{0}{10}{reserved}
100	\rwbits{10}{1}{DFT}
101	\rwbits{11}{1}{SH}
102	\rwbits{12}{1}{OR}
103	\rwbits{13}{1}{ND}
104	\rwbits{14}{1}{SU}
105	\rwbits{15}{1}{LU}
106	\end{tikzpicture}
107	\end{center}
108	\begin{itemize}
109	\item \textbf{DFT:} New DFT result available (see section~\ref{dft}).
110	\item \textbf{SH:} Sweep halted due to halt bit set. Sweep will be resumed once the resume command is issued.
111	\item \textbf{OR:} Data overrun occured (only cleared by resetting the FPGA)
112	\item \textbf{ND:} New data available
113	\item \textbf{SU:} Source unlocked
114	\item \textbf{LU:} LO unlocked
115	\end{itemize}
116	\subsection{Writing a register}
117	Writing a register requires the transfer of two words: First the control word selecting the destination address and a second word containing the new register value:
118	\begin{center}
119	\begin{tikzpicture}
120	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
121	\rwbits{0}{1}{1}
122	\rwbits{1}{1}{0}
123	\rwbits{2}{1}{0}
124	\robits{3}{8}{reserved}
125	\rwbits{11}{5}{Register Address}
126	\end{tikzpicture}
127	\begin{tikzpicture}
128	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
129	\rwbits{0}{16}{Register Value}
130	\end{tikzpicture}
131	\end{center}
132	\subsection{Writing SweepConfig}
133	Initiate the sweep config transfer by sending the command word:
134	\begin{center}
135	\begin{tikzpicture}
136	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
137	\rwbits{0}{1}{0}
138	\rwbits{1}{1}{0}
139	\rwbits{2}{1}{0}
140	\rwbits{3}{13}{Sweep point}
141	\end{tikzpicture}
142	\end{center}
143	The maximum number of points per sweep is 4501, thus the highest valid value for "Sweep point" is 4500. After the control word, send the six words of the sweep config (see section~\ref{sweepconfig}) while keeping NSS low. The sweep config is transmitted MSB first.
144	
145	\subsection{Reading a sampling result}
146	Whenever the ND bit in the interrupt status register is set, new sampling data is available and can be read via SPI. It has to be read before the next sampling data arrives otherwise the old data will be overwritten.
147	
148	Initiate the reading of sampling data by sending the command word:
149	\begin{center}
150	\begin{tikzpicture}
151	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
152	\rwbits{0}{1}{1}
153	\rwbits{1}{1}{1}
154	\rwbits{2}{1}{0}
155	\robits{3}{13}{reserved}
156	\end{tikzpicture}
157	\end{center}
158	Afterwards, read up to 19 words before setting NSS high. These 19 words will contain the sampling result (see section~\ref{result}), transmitted with the least significant word first.
159	
160	\subsection{Resuming a halted sweep}
161	When the halt bit is set in the SweepConfig, the FPGA will configure the Source and LO as requested but will not start the settling timer (and subsequently the sampling process) until this resume command is issued. The halted sweep is indicated by the sweep halted bit in the status register.
162	\begin{center}
163	\begin{tikzpicture}
164	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
165	\rwbits{0}{1}{0}
166	\rwbits{1}{1}{0}
167	\rwbits{2}{1}{1}
168	\robits{3}{13}{reserved}
169	\end{tikzpicture}
170	\end{center}
171	
172	\subsection{Reading ADC limits}
173	The FPGA keeps track of the highest and lowest sample of each ADC to detect saturation and verify signal levels.
174	
175	Initiate the reading of ADC limit data by sending the command word:
176	\begin{center}
177	\begin{tikzpicture}
178	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
179	\rwbits{0}{1}{1}
180	\rwbits{1}{1}{1}
181	\rwbits{2}{1}{1}
182	\robits{3}{13}{reserved}
183	\end{tikzpicture}
184	\end{center}
185	Afterwards, read 6 words before setting NSS high. These 6 words will contain the sampling result:
186	
187	\begin{center}
188	\begin{tikzpicture}
189	\bitrect{16}{96-\bit}
190	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 minimum}
191	\end{tikzpicture}
192	\begin{tikzpicture}
193	\bitrect{16}{80-\bit}
194	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 maximum}
195	\end{tikzpicture}
196	\begin{tikzpicture}
197	\bitrect{16}{64-\bit}
198	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 minimum}
199	\end{tikzpicture}
200	\begin{tikzpicture}
201	\bitrect{16}{48-\bit}
202	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 maximum}
203	\end{tikzpicture}
204	\begin{tikzpicture}
205	\bitrect{16}{32-\bit}
206	\rwbits{0}{16}{Reference minimum}
207	\end{tikzpicture}
208	\begin{tikzpicture}
209	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
210	\rwbits{0}{16}{Reference maximum}
211	\end{tikzpicture}
212	\end{center}
213	
214	\subsection{Resetting the ADC limit}
215	Issuing this command result in all minimum values set to 32767 and all maximum values set to -32768.
216	\begin{center}
217	\begin{tikzpicture}
218	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
219	\rwbits{0}{1}{0}
220	\rwbits{1}{1}{1}
221	\rwbits{2}{1}{1}
222	\robits{3}{13}{reserved}
223	\end{tikzpicture}
224	\end{center}
225	
226	\subsection{Reading the DFT result}
227	Initiate the reading of the DFT result (see section~\ref{dft}) by sending the command word:
228	\begin{center}
229	\begin{tikzpicture}
230	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
231	\rwbits{0}{1}{1}
232	\rwbits{1}{1}{0}
233	\rwbits{2}{1}{1}
234	\robits{3}{13}{reserved}
235	\end{tikzpicture}
236	\end{center}
237	Afterwards, read 12 words before setting NSS high. These 12 words will contain the first bin of the DFT, the least significant word is transmitted first:
238	\begin{center}
239	\begin{tikzpicture}
240	\bitrect{16}{192-\bit}
241	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 I[47:32]}
242	\end{tikzpicture}
243	\begin{tikzpicture}
244	\bitrect{16}{176-\bit}
245	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 I[31:16]}
246	\end{tikzpicture}
247	\begin{tikzpicture}
248	\bitrect{16}{168-\bit}
249	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 I[15:0]}
250	\end{tikzpicture}
251	\begin{tikzpicture}
252	\bitrect{16}{144-\bit}
253	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 Q[47:32]}
254	\end{tikzpicture}
255	\begin{tikzpicture}
256	\bitrect{16}{128-\bit}
257	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 Q[31:16]}
258	\end{tikzpicture}
259	\begin{tikzpicture}
260	\bitrect{16}{112-\bit}
261	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 1 Q[15:0]}
262	\end{tikzpicture}
263	\begin{tikzpicture}
264	\bitrect{16}{96-\bit}
265	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 I[47:32]}
266	\end{tikzpicture}
267	\begin{tikzpicture}
268	\bitrect{16}{80-\bit}
269	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 I[31:16]}
270	\end{tikzpicture}
271	\begin{tikzpicture}
272	\bitrect{16}{64-\bit}
273	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 I[15:0]}
274	\end{tikzpicture}
275	\begin{tikzpicture}
276	\bitrect{16}{48-\bit}
277	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 Q[47:32]}
278	\end{tikzpicture}
279	\begin{tikzpicture}
280	\bitrect{16}{32-\bit}
281	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 Q[31:16]}
282	\end{tikzpicture}
283	\begin{tikzpicture}
284	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
285	\rwbits{0}{16}{Port 2 Q[15:0]}
286	\end{tikzpicture}
287	\end{center}
288	Repeating this procedure will return the next DFT bin. For each bin, the CS pin has to be toggled and the command word needs to be sent again (each DFT bin requires a new SPI transaction). The DFT interrupt is reset once all bins have been read. Alternatively, toggling the DFT off and on (by disabling and enabling its interrupt) will also reset the interrupt flag.
289	
290	\section{Registers}
291	\subsection{Interrupt Mask Register: 0x00}
292	\begin{center}
293	\begin{tikzpicture}
294	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
295	\robits{0}{10}{reserved}
296	\rwbits{10}{1}{DFTIE}
297	\rwbits{11}{1}{SHIE}
298	\rwbits{12}{1}{ORIE}
299	\rwbits{13}{1}{NDIE}
300	\rwbits{14}{1}{SUIE}
301	\rwbits{15}{1}{LUIE}
302	\end{tikzpicture}
303	\end{center}
304	\begin{itemize}
305	\item \textbf{DFTIE:} DFT interrupt enable. This bit also enables the DFT (see section~\ref{dft}).
306	\item \textbf{SHIE:} Sweep halted interrupt enable
307	\item \textbf{ORIE:} Data overrun interrupt enable 
308	\item \textbf{NDIE:} New data interrupt enable
309	\item \textbf{SUIE:} Source unlocked interrupt enable
310	\item \textbf{LUIE:} LO unlocked interrupt enable 
311	\end{itemize}
312	
313	\subsection{Sweep Points Register: 0x01}
314	\begin{center}
315	\begin{tikzpicture}
316	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
317	\rwbits{0}{16}{Number of points per sweep - 1}
318	\end{tikzpicture}
319	\end{center}
320	The register contains the number of points per sweep negative one, e.g. set to 11b if the sweep contains four points.
321	
322	\subsection{Samples Per Point Register: 0x02}
323	\label{reg:spp}
324	\begin{center}
325	\begin{tikzpicture}
326	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
327	\robits{0}{3}{reserved}
328	\rwbits{3}{13}{SPP[12:0]}
329	\end{tikzpicture}
330	\end{center}
331	\begin{itemize}
332	\item \textbf{SPP[12:0]:} The register contains the number of samples per point in increments of 16 samples (e.g. SPP=0b0000001000=0x08 uses 128 samples per point). The value of this register is only used if SweepConfig[92:90] is set to 000. Otherwise it is overwritten for the sweep point with one of seven preselected values.
333	\end{itemize}
334	
335	\subsection{System Control Register: 0x03}
336	\begin{center}
337	\begin{tikzpicture}
338	\footnotesize
339	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
340	\rwbits{0}{1}{P1EN}
341	\rwbits{1}{1}{P2EN}
342	\rwbits{2}{1}{REN}
343	\rwbits{3}{1}{AMEN}
344	\rwbits{4}{1}{SOEN}
345	\rwbits{5}{1}{LOEN}
346	\rwbits{6}{1}{RLED}
347	\rwbits{7}{1}{LED6}
348	\rwbits{8}{1}{LED7}
349	\rwbits{9}{2}{Window[1:0]}
350	\rwbits{11}{1}{SCEN}
351	\rwbits{12}{1}{LCEN}
352	\robits{13}{1}{reserved}
353	%\rwbits{13}{1}{EXP2}
354	\rwbits{14}{1}{SYM}
355	\rwbits{15}{1}{PSEN}
356	\end{tikzpicture}
357	\end{center}
358	\begin{itemize}
359	\item \textbf{P1EN:} Port 1 Mixers/Amplifier enable
360	\item \textbf{P2EN:} Port 2 Mixers/Amplifier enable
361	\item \textbf{REN:} Reference Mixers/Amplifier enable
362	\item \textbf{AMEN:} Source amplifier enable
363	\item \textbf{SOEN:} Source enable
364	\item \textbf{LOEN:} LO enable
365	\item \textbf{RLED:} External frequency LED control
366	\item \textbf{LED6:}{User LED 6 control}
367	\item \textbf{LED7:}{User LED 7 control}
368	\item \textbf{Window[1:0]:}{Type of window to be used in calculation of real/imag of the sampling result}
369	\begin{center}
370	\begin{tabular}{ c|c }
371	Setting & Window type\\
372	 \hline
373	00 & Rectangular (no window)\\
374	01 & Kaiser\\
375	10 & Hann\\
376	11 & Flat Top\\
377	\end{tabular}
378	\end{center}
379	\item \textbf{SCEN:}{Source chip enable}
380	\item \textbf{LCEN:}{LO chip enable}
381	%\item \textbf{EXP1:}{Excite Port1 during sweep}
382	\item \textbf{SYM:}{Sync master, see also section~\ref{synchronization}. If multiple devices are combined, exactly one must have this bit set.}
383	\item \textbf{PSEN:}{Port switch enable}
384	\end{itemize}
385	
386	\subsection{ADC Prescaler register: 0x04}
387	\label{reg:ADC}
388	\begin{center}
389	\begin{tikzpicture}
390	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
391	\robits{0}{8}{reserved}
392	\rwbits{8}{8}{Presc[7:0]}
393	\end{tikzpicture}
394	\end{center}
395	\begin{itemize}
396	\item \textbf{Presc[7:0]:} Amount of FPGA clock cycles between ADC samples.
397	$$ SR_{ADC} = \frac{\SI{102.4}{\mega\hertz}}{Presc} $$
398	The minimum value for this register is 112, which results in a samplerate of roughly \SI{914.3}{\kilo\hertz}. If Presc is set to a lower value, the data acquisition from the ADC is not done when the next sample starts and samples will be skipped.
399	\end{itemize}
400	
401	\subsection{Phase Increment: 0x05}
402	\label{reg:phaseinc}
403	\begin{center}
404	\begin{tikzpicture}
405	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
406	\robits{0}{4}{reserved}
407	\rwbits{4}{12}{Phase Increment[11:0]}
408	\end{tikzpicture}
409	\end{center}
410	\begin{itemize}
411	\item \textbf{Phase Increment[7:0]:} Phase angle between ADC samples for DFT bin calculation in $\frac{2\pi}{4096}$rad.
412	For a given ADC samplerate $SR_{ADC}$ and final IF frequency $f_{IF2}$ set this value to
413	$$ PhaseInc = \frac{4096 * f_{IF2}}{SR_{ADC}} $$
414	For the the default IF frequency of $f_{IF2} = \SI{250}{\kilo\hertz}$ this evaluates to 10*Presc (see ADC prescaler register).
415	\end{itemize}
416	
417	\subsection{Sweep setup: 0x06}
418	Each point in the sweep is done in stages. Each stage consists of (optionally) routing the source signal to one of the ports and sampling of all ADCs. A "new data" interrupt is triggered after each stage.
419	\label{reg:sweepsetup}
420	\begin{center}
421	\begin{tikzpicture}
422	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
423	\rwbits{0}{3}{Stages}
424	\rwbits{3}{1}{SYNC}
425	\robits{4}{6}{reserved}
426	\rwbits{10}{3}{Port 1 stage}
427	\rwbits{13}{3}{Port 2 stage}
428	\end{tikzpicture}
429	\end{center}
430	\begin{itemize}
431	\item \textbf{Stages} Number of stages per point - 1. Normally the number of stages is equal to the number of ports but it can also be less (e.g. if only S11 is measured).
432	\item \textbf{SYNC:} Enables synchronization mode (see section~\ref{synchronization}).
433	\item \textbf{Port 1 stage} Number of stage during which the source signal is routed to port 1. Must not have the same value as Port 2 stage.
434	\item \textbf{Port 2 stage} Number of stage during which the source signal is routed to port 2. Must not have the same value as Port 1 stage.
435	\end{itemize}
436	
437	\subsection{Hardware override register: 0x07}
438	Allows overwriting hardware settings, regardless of whether a sweep is active or not.
439	\label{reg:hw_override}
440	\begin{center}
441	\begin{tikzpicture}
442	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
443	\rwbits{0}{1}{OWE}
444	\rwbits{1}{7}{Attenuator[6:0]}
445	\rwbits{8}{2}{SourceFilter}
446	\rwbits{10}{1}{BS}
447	\rwbits{11}{1}{P1EN}
448	\rwbits{12}{1}{P2EN}
449	\robits{13}{3}{reserved}
450	\end{tikzpicture}
451	\end{center}
452	\begin{itemize}
453	\item \textbf{OWE:} Overwrite enable. If 1, this register is used to configure the hardware. If 0, all other bits in the register are ignored. Must be set to 0 for valid sweep operation.
454	\item \textbf{Attenuator[6:0]:} Attenuator setting
455	\item \textbf{SourceFilter:} Low pass filter selection for source signal
456	\begin{center}
457	\begin{tabular}{ c|c }
458	Setting & Selected Band\\
459	 \hline
460	00 & \SIrange{0}{900}{\mega\hertz}\\
461	01 & \SIrange{900}{1800}{\mega\hertz}\\
462	10 & \SIrange{1800}{3500}{\mega\hertz}\\
463	11 & \SIrange{3500}{6000}{\mega\hertz}\\
464	\end{tabular}
465	\end{center}
466	\item \textbf{BS: Band select.} Set to 0 for highband, set to 1 for lowband.
467	\item \textbf{P1EN:} Route signal to port 1. Must not be enabled at the same time as P2EN.
468	\item \textbf{P2EN:} Route signal to port 2. Must not be enabled at the same time as P1EN.
469	\end{itemize}
470	
471	\subsection{MAX2871 Default Values Registers: 0x08-0x0F}
472	See datasheet of MAX2871 for bit descriptions. Bits for the fields N, FRAC, M, VCO and DIV\_A are "don't care" as they will be overwritten by the SweepConfig setting.
473	\begin{center}
474	\begin{tikzpicture}
475	\bitrect{16}{16-\bit}
476	\rwbits{0}{16}{MAX2871 Register 0[15:0]}
477	\end{tikzpicture}
478	\begin{tikzpicture}
